diff --git a/plume_device_thread.h b/plume_device_thread.h
index 5d3edc6..0a35ced 100644
--- a/plume_device_thread.h
+++ b/plume_device_thread.h
@@ -12,7 +12,12 @@
 //
 // - A call that returns something (create*(), map(), a swap chain's resize() and needsResize(), ...) runs on the
 //   device thread while the caller waits. map() of a buffer whose data the backend delivers asynchronously waits on
-//   the calling side until isMapReady(), so the device thread keeps returning to its event loop meanwhile.
+//   the calling side until isMapReady(), so the device thread keeps returning to its event loop meanwhile. Its polls
+//   are 100 us sleeps, which a browser's thread rounds up to about 2 ms.
+// - An UPLOAD or GPU_UPLOAD buffer is written through a copy on the calling side: map() returns it at once, and
+//   unmap() queues what was written (the range given, or all of it) to the backend's own map() and unmap(). So such a
+//   buffer must be written between map() and unmap(), and what is written reaches the backend at unmap(). A call
+//   waiting for the device thread costs some 50-100 us in a browser, and RT64 maps a few dozen upload buffers a frame.
 // - A call that returns nothing (unmap(), setName(), descriptor writes, executeCommandLists(), present(), destroying
 //   an object) is queued and the caller goes on. The queue is first in, first out, so whatever happened before a call
 //   on the calling side, under the caller's own locks or on another thread it waited for, reaches the backend first.
